{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: CRA \u2014 TD1 2026 \u00b7 Line 44800 \u00b7 Required tax instalments \u00b7 2026 federal & Ontario rates \u00b7 Example estimate, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Maya worked two jobs last year. Both employers took tax off every single paycheque."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Both did exactly what they were told to do."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And in April, Maya's income tax bill was six thousand, five hundred and twenty-three dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So how can two employers both get it right, and still get it wrong? The answer is one box, on one form."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And there's a second trap, one that can follow Maya into next year. Stay with me, we'll get to both."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Every number here uses twenty twenty-six federal and Ontario rates, for one example person. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Meet Maya. Ontario. Single. A day job that pays fifty-five thousand dollars a year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And an evening job that pays thirty-five thousand more. Ninety thousand in total. Our example, not a real person."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "On the first day at each job, Maya filled out the same form. The T D one, the personal tax credits return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "And on both forms, Maya claimed the basic personal amount. On a new job, that feels completely normal."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Here's the thing about payroll. Each employer only sees its own job."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The day job sees fifty-five thousand dollars. It has no idea the evening job exists."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The evening job sees thirty-five thousand. It has no idea about the day job."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So each one withholds tax as if its job were Maya's whole life."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Our estimate: the day job withholds about seven thousand, one hundred and seventy-eight dollars of income tax. The evening job, about three thousand, three hundred and thirty-two."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Ten thousand, five hundred and ten dollars in total. Every paycheque looked fine."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But the tax return doesn't look at jobs. It looks at Maya."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Ninety thousand dollars of income, on one return. The tax on that: seventeen thousand and thirty-four dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Seventeen thousand and thirty-four owed, ten thousand, five hundred and ten withheld. The difference is the bill. Six thousand, five hundred and twenty-three."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "About five hundred and forty-four dollars for every month of the year that nobody set aside."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So where exactly did it come from? Two places."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Leak number one: the personal amounts were counted twice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The basic personal amount is meant to be used once per person. Federally, sixteen thousand, four hundred and fifty-two dollars, at fourteen percent. In Ontario, twelve thousand, nine hundred and eighty-nine, at five point oh five percent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Claimed at both jobs, those credits reduced Maya's withholding twice. Together, about two thousand, nine hundred and fifty-nine dollars of tax that was never taken off."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Leak number two is quieter, and it's bigger."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The evening job thinks thirty-five thousand dollars is Maya's entire income. So it withholds at the lowest rates: fourteen percent federal, five point oh five percent Ontario."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But on the return, those thirty-five thousand dollars sit on top of the day job. They land in higher brackets: twenty point five percent federal, and nine point one five in Ontario."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "About nineteen percent withheld on those dollars, up to almost thirty percent owed. That gap: about three thousand, five hundred and sixty-four dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Two thousand, nine hundred and fifty-nine, plus three thousand, five hundred and sixty-four. That's the six thousand, five hundred and twenty-three."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So here's the question people always ask. Does it matter how big the second job is?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Let's run two more versions of Maya. Same day job, but the evening job pays only ten thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "On its own, ten thousand dollars is below the basic personal amount. So the evening job withholds nothing. Zero income tax, all year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But on the return, those ten thousand dollars still sit on top of fifty-five thousand. The bill: about two thousand, five hundred and forty-eight dollars. From a job that never took a cent of tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Version three: two equal jobs. Forty-five thousand dollars each. Same ninety thousand in total."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Each employer withholds as if forty-five thousand were everything. Together, about ten thousand, four hundred and eighty-six dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The tax on ninety thousand is still seventeen thousand and thirty-four. The bill: about six thousand, five hundred and forty-eight. Almost exactly the same as before."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So it's not the size of the second job that creates the bill. It's the fact that each payroll only sees part of the picture."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Now, one piece of good news on the return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "E I premiums stop at sixty-eight thousand, nine hundred dollars of earnings for the year. But each employer deducts E I on its own pay."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Maya paid one thousand, four hundred and sixty-seven dollars of E I. The yearly maximum is one thousand, one hundred and twenty-three. About three hundred and forty-four dollars comes back."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "C P P works the same way: when more than one employer deducts it, the return checks what you actually had to contribute, and an overpayment is refunded."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So a little comes back. But not six thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So, the one box on one form."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The T D one has a box on page two: more than one employer or payer at the same time."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The C R A's rule: if you've already claimed your personal amounts with one employer, you can't claim them again with another. You tick that box, and enter zero as your total claim."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Do that at the evening job, and leak number one is closed. That's about two thousand, nine hundred and fifty-nine dollars handled through payroll."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But leak number two is still open. The evening job still doesn't know it's sitting on top of fifty-five thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For that, the T D one has a second tool: additional tax to be deducted. You choose an extra amount to come off every pay."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "In Maya's case, about three thousand, five hundred dollars a year. Spread over twenty-six pay periods, roughly a hundred and thirty-seven dollars a cheque. That's our estimate. Your own number depends on your pay."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Now, the second trap. The one that follows you into next year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The C R A asks for tax instalments when your net tax owing is more than three thousand dollars this year, and also in either of the two previous years."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Maya's balance was over three thousand. If the next year looks the same, instalments can be required, with payments due March fifteenth, June fifteenth, September fifteenth and December fifteenth."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So fixing the forms now doesn't just avoid one bill. It keeps one bad year from turning into a schedule."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And this isn't only about two jobs."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The T D one rule is for anyone with more than one employer or payer at the same time. Each payer only sees its own payments."
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "If a balance owing shows up every April, check how many payers you had, and what each one thinks your income is."
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So, what happened to Maya?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Both employers followed the rules they were given. The forms told each one that Maya had a single job."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Fix one: claim your personal amounts with one employer only. Tick the box at the other job, and enter zero."
  },
  {
   "id": "L60",
   "file": "vo/L60.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Fix two: ask for additional tax to be deducted, so the second job withholds at your real rate."
  },
  {
   "id": "L61",
   "file": "vo/L61.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Fix three: if you already owe, look at whether instalment reminders could follow."
  },
  {
   "id": "L62",
   "file": "vo/L62.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: the same one hundred thousand dollar salary in Alberta, Manitoba and Ontario. One of them keeps thousands more. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
